1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,310
Imagine thousands of tonnes of junk
flying around Earth. Dead satellites.

2
00:00:05,930 --> 00:00:11,290
Spent rocket stages. Fragments left
behind by explosions and collisions.

3
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:16,260
And sometimes, pieces of metal so
small that they are difficult to track.

4
00:00:17,060 --> 00:00:20,320
All of them can be travelling at
several kilometres per second.

5
00:00:21,310 --> 00:00:26,450
Space is enormous. So how can a little
bit of garbage possibly be a problem?

6
00:00:27,070 --> 00:00:29,190
Because in space, a
piece of garbage doesn't

7
00:00:29,420 --> 00:00:31,890
have to be large to cause enormous damage.

8
00:00:32,490 --> 00:00:34,050
It just has to hit something.

9
00:00:34,590 --> 00:00:38,590
And when it does, it can create
hundreds or thousands of new pieces.

10
00:00:39,370 --> 00:00:41,570
So we have a rather strange problem.

11
00:00:41,890 --> 00:00:46,470
How do you clean a place where there is
no floor, no broom, and no garbage truck?

12
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:50,100
On Earth, leaving
something behind is easy.

13
00:00:51,360 --> 00:00:53,100
Eventually, gravity brings it down.

14
00:00:53,780 --> 00:00:56,520
In orbit, that is not
necessarily what happens.

15
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:59,540
A satellite in low Earth
orbit can travel at

16
00:00:59,770 --> 00:01:02,280
roughly seven to eight
kilometres per second.

17
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:06,440
That is more than twenty-five
thousand kilometres per hour.

18
00:01:06,970 --> 00:01:08,850
And two objects travelling
in different orbits

19
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:11,650
can collide at even
higher relative speeds.

20
00:01:12,550 --> 00:01:15,250
So a tiny screw, a
fragment of insulation, or

21
00:01:15,480 --> 00:01:18,010
a piece of metal is not
really "just a screw."

22
00:01:18,490 --> 00:01:20,030
It is a projectile.

23
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:23,200
And that is why some pieces
of space debris can remain

24
00:01:23,430 --> 00:01:26,580
in orbit for decades,
centuries, or even longer.

25
00:01:27,280 --> 00:01:29,200
It depends on how high they are.

26
00:01:29,660 --> 00:01:33,360
The higher the orbit, the less
atmospheric drag they experience.

27
00:01:34,020 --> 00:01:35,940
And the longer they can stay there.

28
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:38,760
This is where things get complicated.

29
00:01:39,680 --> 00:01:41,440
Let's say we have a dead satellite.

30
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:44,120
We could simply send
another spacecraft to it.

31
00:01:44,760 --> 00:01:46,000
The spacecraft catches it.

32
00:01:46,230 --> 00:01:49,440
And brings it down. Problem solved.

33
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:51,740
Except it is not that simple.

34
00:01:52,590 --> 00:01:56,010
The cleanup spacecraft first
has to reach a similar orbit.

35
00:01:56,670 --> 00:02:00,670
It has to approach the target at the
right speed and from the right direction.

36
00:02:01,330 --> 00:02:03,390
And it may have to
approach a satellite that

37
00:02:03,620 --> 00:02:05,950
is tumbling uncontrollably through space.

38
00:02:07,190 --> 00:02:12,150
Many old satellites were never designed
to be captured. They don't have handles.

39
00:02:12,230 --> 00:02:13,810
They don't have docking ports.

40
00:02:14,530 --> 00:02:18,150
And they may not have any functioning
systems left to help control them.

41
00:02:18,470 --> 00:02:20,730
It is basically a flying wreck.

42
00:02:21,390 --> 00:02:24,590
And now imagine trying to
attach another spacecraft to it

43
00:02:24,670 --> 00:02:27,810
while both are moving at
several kilometres per second.

44
00:02:28,610 --> 00:02:32,030
That is why active debris-removal
missions are so difficult.

45
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:36,580
Europe's ClearSpace-1 mission, for
example, is designed to capture a

46
00:02:36,810 --> 00:02:41,040
non-functional object in orbit and bring
it down together with the spacecraft.

47
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:44,780
But what about the enormous
number of smaller pieces?

48
00:02:45,580 --> 00:02:47,620
For those, we need other ideas.

49
00:02:48,680 --> 00:02:51,860
This sounds reasonable. We
have an object we don't want.

50
00:02:52,090 --> 00:02:53,560
So we destroy it.

51
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:56,340
But that could make
the problem much worse.

52
00:02:57,050 --> 00:02:59,070
If you smash one large
piece of debris into

53
00:02:59,300 --> 00:03:02,290
hundreds of smaller pieces,
you haven't removed it.

54
00:03:02,490 --> 00:03:04,810
You have created a cloud of debris.

55
00:03:05,430 --> 00:03:08,510
And every one of those pieces
can collide with something else.

56
00:03:09,050 --> 00:03:11,130
That can create even more fragments.

57
00:03:11,290 --> 00:03:13,690
And the cycle can continue.

58
00:03:14,290 --> 00:03:18,570
This is the basic idea behind the
scenario known as the Kessler syndrome.

59
00:03:19,590 --> 00:03:22,990
A chain reaction of collisions
could create so much debris that

60
00:03:23,220 --> 00:03:27,150
some orbits become increasingly
difficult or dangerous to use.

61
00:03:27,810 --> 00:03:29,950
So if we want to clean up space, we cannot

62
00:03:30,180 --> 00:03:32,850
simply smash everything
into smaller pieces.

63
00:03:34,040 --> 00:03:37,520
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

64
00:03:38,140 --> 00:03:39,760
It really helps the channel.

65
00:03:40,530 --> 00:03:44,070
Do we actually have to carry it down? No.

66
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:46,690
And this is where the first trick appears.

67
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:50,590
We don't necessarily have to
catch the debris and tow it away.

68
00:03:51,070 --> 00:03:53,010
We just have to change its orbit.

69
00:03:53,580 --> 00:03:56,320
If we lower the lowest point
of an object's orbit enough,

70
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:58,920
it can begin passing through
the upper atmosphere.

71
00:03:59,860 --> 00:04:01,680
The atmosphere creates drag.

72
00:04:02,460 --> 00:04:06,280
The object loses energy.
Its orbit gets lower.

73
00:04:07,040 --> 00:04:09,520
Eventually, it enters
the denser atmosphere.

74
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:12,900
And many small objects
burn up on the way down.

75
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:15,240
So the problem suddenly changes.

76
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:19,420
It is no longer: "How do we get
this satellite back to Earth?"

77
00:04:19,800 --> 00:04:21,880
It is: "How do we give it a tiny push?"

78
00:04:22,110 --> 00:04:26,160
And there are several surprisingly
strange ways to do that.

79
00:04:26,640 --> 00:04:29,240
The most obvious solution
is still a robot.

80
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:33,080
Send a spacecraft to the
dead satellite. Grab it.

81
00:04:33,310 --> 00:04:34,800
And change its orbit.

82
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:39,880
The advantage is that this can work
even with relatively large objects.

83
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:43,840
The disadvantage is that every
piece of debris is different.

84
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:46,300
Every mission costs money.

85
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:49,340
Every target requires
navigation, rendezvous,

86
00:04:49,570 --> 00:04:52,040
and some way of safely attaching to it.

87
00:04:52,610 --> 00:04:54,750
And if there are hundreds
or thousands of objects

88
00:04:54,980 --> 00:04:57,910
we want to remove, we would
need a lot of missions.

89
00:04:58,490 --> 00:05:02,730
So perhaps we need something that doesn't
have to fly to every single piece.

90
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:06,960
Yes. A net. The idea
is surprisingly simple.

91
00:05:07,580 --> 00:05:11,140
A spacecraft approaches the
target and deploys a large net.

92
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:13,560
The net catches the object.

93
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:17,800
The spacecraft can then change its
orbit and bring the target down.

94
00:05:18,730 --> 00:05:20,510
The advantage is that
you don't necessarily

95
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:23,650
need a precisely designed
attachment point.

96
00:05:23,990 --> 00:05:26,210
You just need to catch the object.

97
00:05:26,940 --> 00:05:28,680
But there is a small problem.

98
00:05:29,240 --> 00:05:31,340
You are deploying a large
net while both spacecraft

99
00:05:31,570 --> 00:05:35,000
and debris are moving at
several kilometres per second.

100
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:39,080
And you really don't want your net to
become tangled with something else.

101
00:05:39,600 --> 00:05:42,000
So even this apparently
simple idea requires

102
00:05:42,230 --> 00:05:45,140
extremely precise navigation and control.

103
00:05:45,820 --> 00:05:50,880
Now we get to a solution that sounds
like science fiction. A laser.

104
00:05:51,300 --> 00:05:54,400
But not because we are going
to blast the satellite apart.

105
00:05:54,980 --> 00:05:58,000
That would be exactly the
problem we are trying to avoid.

106
00:05:58,660 --> 00:06:01,660
Instead, a laser could give
the object a tiny push.

107
00:06:02,240 --> 00:06:03,800
There are two main ideas.

108
00:06:04,620 --> 00:06:07,000
The first uses radiation pressure.

109
00:06:07,540 --> 00:06:10,480
Photons have no rest mass,
but they carry momentum.

110
00:06:11,480 --> 00:06:14,900
When light hits an object, it
transfers some of that momentum.

111
00:06:15,702 --> 00:06:17,502
Normally, the effect is tiny.

112
00:06:17,582 --> 00:06:20,542
But in space, we can potentially apply it

113
00:06:20,772 --> 00:06:23,562
very precisely and over
a long period of time.

114
00:06:24,242 --> 00:06:28,422
The second idea is much more
interesting. Laser ablation.

115
00:06:28,982 --> 00:06:32,082
A powerful laser heats a
tiny area of the object's

116
00:06:32,312 --> 00:06:34,322
surface until some of
the material vaporises.

117
00:06:35,432 --> 00:06:38,112
That material shoots
away from the surface.

118
00:06:38,592 --> 00:06:41,332
And because of the conservation
of momentum, the object

119
00:06:41,562 --> 00:06:44,712
receives a small impulse
in the opposite direction.

120
00:06:45,452 --> 00:06:48,792
In other words, it is
basically a tiny rocket engine.

121
00:06:49,432 --> 00:06:51,772
Except the fuel is the object itself.

122
00:06:51,932 --> 00:06:55,152
And the laser doesn't have
to physically touch it.

123
00:06:55,581 --> 00:06:57,601
Why could a laser actually work?

124
00:06:58,121 --> 00:07:02,141
Because we don't need to change the
object's speed by kilometres per second.

125
00:07:02,761 --> 00:07:05,321
We only need to change
it by a tiny amount.

126
00:07:06,331 --> 00:07:08,591
Imagine a ball moving around Earth.

127
00:07:09,151 --> 00:07:11,351
Give it a very small change in velocity.

128
00:07:11,791 --> 00:07:14,391
You might not notice anything immediately.

129
00:07:14,811 --> 00:07:17,531
But its orbit is now slightly different.

130
00:07:17,851 --> 00:07:21,691
And after many orbits, that tiny
difference can become significant.

131
00:07:22,741 --> 00:07:26,121
The object's lowest point can
move into the upper atmosphere.

132
00:07:26,841 --> 00:07:28,841
And then the atmosphere takes over.

133
00:07:29,421 --> 00:07:32,001
That is what makes the
idea so interesting.

134
00:07:32,841 --> 00:07:35,081
The laser doesn't have
to destroy the debris.

135
00:07:35,741 --> 00:07:37,721
It just has to give it a little push.

136
00:07:38,821 --> 00:07:41,561
So why don't we have giant
space lasers already?

137
00:07:41,821 --> 00:07:46,221
Because, of course, there are
problems. The first is accuracy.

138
00:07:46,701 --> 00:07:49,261
We have to know exactly
where the object is.

139
00:07:49,341 --> 00:07:53,221
And that is particularly difficult
for small pieces of debris.

140
00:07:54,221 --> 00:07:56,121
The second problem is energy.

141
00:07:56,921 --> 00:08:00,121
Ablation requires a
sufficiently intense laser beam.

142
00:08:00,861 --> 00:08:02,821
The third problem is the atmosphere.

143
00:08:03,601 --> 00:08:06,281
If we are firing a laser from
the ground, the beam has to pass

144
00:08:06,511 --> 00:08:10,021
through Earth's atmosphere,
which can scatter and distort it.

145
00:08:10,571 --> 00:08:13,051
And the fourth problem
may be the most important.

146
00:08:13,551 --> 00:08:15,771
What if we get the direction wrong?

147
00:08:16,311 --> 00:08:19,111
With objects moving this
fast, even a small error

148
00:08:19,341 --> 00:08:21,631
can produce a different
orbit than intended.

149
00:08:22,471 --> 00:08:25,651
So a laser is not a giant
space vacuum cleaner.

150
00:08:26,331 --> 00:08:29,391
It is more like an extremely
precise orbital brake.

151
00:08:30,079 --> 00:08:31,839
What about the really big stuff?

152
00:08:32,459 --> 00:08:34,999
This is where lasers
become less attractive.

153
00:08:35,719 --> 00:08:38,119
If you have a satellite
weighing several tonnes,

154
00:08:38,199 --> 00:08:41,019
changing its orbit
requires a lot more energy.

155
00:08:41,759 --> 00:08:45,979
So for large objects, a robotic
spacecraft may make much more sense.

156
00:08:46,819 --> 00:08:49,899
For smaller debris, a laser
could potentially be useful.

157
00:08:50,579 --> 00:08:54,659
For objects that can be captured, we
could use nets or other capture systems.

158
00:08:55,389 --> 00:08:58,309
And for future satellites, the
best solution may be to design

159
00:08:58,539 --> 00:09:01,949
them so they can safely leave
orbit at the end of their lives.

160
00:09:02,609 --> 00:09:04,329
Because the cheapest
piece of space junk...

161
00:09:04,559 --> 00:09:08,009
is the piece that never
becomes space junk.

162
00:09:08,589 --> 00:09:12,049
Space debris is not only
something created decades ago.

163
00:09:12,289 --> 00:09:14,829
Every year, we launch more spacecraft.

164
00:09:15,069 --> 00:09:18,589
And the number of objects
around Earth continues to grow.

165
00:09:19,279 --> 00:09:22,059
So we cannot solve the
problem simply by cleaning.

166
00:09:22,679 --> 00:09:25,399
We also have to prevent new
debris from accumulating.

167
00:09:26,379 --> 00:09:29,599
That means things like
reliable end-of-life disposal.

168
00:09:29,719 --> 00:09:33,299
Tracking objects. Avoiding collisions.

169
00:09:34,139 --> 00:09:37,819
And designing spacecraft with
their eventual disposal in mind.

170
00:09:38,579 --> 00:09:42,019
Otherwise, we would end up
in a rather absurd situation.

171
00:09:42,459 --> 00:09:45,859
We would build increasingly
sophisticated vacuum cleaners...

172
00:09:45,939 --> 00:09:49,259
while continuing to throw
garbage onto the floor.

173
00:09:50,129 --> 00:09:52,289
So, can we actually clean up space?

174
00:09:52,929 --> 00:09:55,169
Probably not with one magical invention.

175
00:09:55,869 --> 00:09:58,089
There probably won't be
a single giant machine

176
00:09:58,319 --> 00:10:00,869
that circles Earth and
collects everything.

177
00:10:01,409 --> 00:10:04,189
Instead, we will need a
combination of technologies.

178
00:10:05,529 --> 00:10:06,849
Robots for large objects.

179
00:10:07,079 --> 00:10:10,389
Capture systems for suitable targets.

180
00:10:10,469 --> 00:10:13,109
Lasers for some smaller pieces.

181
00:10:13,709 --> 00:10:15,329
Better tracking of objects.

182
00:10:16,009 --> 00:10:19,129
And, perhaps most importantly,
rules and engineering practices

183
00:10:19,359 --> 00:10:22,749
that prevent new debris from
being created in the first place.

184
00:10:23,409 --> 00:10:26,409
Because in space, we don't
always have to remove an object.

185
00:10:27,229 --> 00:10:29,349
Sometimes, we just have to push it.

186
00:10:29,889 --> 00:10:32,269
Maybe by a hundred metres per second.

187
00:10:32,989 --> 00:10:35,189
Barely more than one percent of its speed.

188
00:10:35,469 --> 00:10:38,449
Then gravity and the
atmosphere can do the rest.

189
00:10:39,099 --> 00:10:41,199
But these are only some of the ideas.

190
00:10:41,279 --> 00:10:44,919
Because lasers, robots, and
nets are not the only strange

191
00:10:45,149 --> 00:10:48,239
ways we could deal with the
junk orbiting our planet.

192
00:10:49,199 --> 00:10:52,579
There are concepts involving
harpoons, giant sails, magnetic

193
00:10:52,809 --> 00:10:57,499
fields, electrodynamic tethers,
and even using sunlight itself.

194
00:10:58,239 --> 00:11:01,279
So in the next episode, we will
look at some of the other ways

195
00:11:01,509 --> 00:11:05,339
humanity is trying to answer an
absurd question: How do you clean

196
00:11:05,569 --> 00:11:08,729
up something moving at twenty-eight
thousand kilometres per hour?
